\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Spin diagonalization on even tori}
\date{2026-11-23}

\p{Let #{S} be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex Hermitian
Clifford module with self-adjoint Euclidean generators
#{\varepsilon_j^S}. Take the \vocabk{staggered operator}{fdip-014Q}
for #{n\ge1}, #{a>0}, even #{N_j\ge3}, and any unitary
connection on #{F}. In the centered operator of
the \vocabk{spaced Wilson construction}{fdip-009U}, use these
generators on #{S}. Define #{\Upsilon} at site #{x} to act by
#{\Upsilon_x\otimes\bbI_F}, where
##{\Upsilon_x=(\varepsilon_1^S)^{x_1}\cdots
(\varepsilon_n^S)^{x_n}.}
This frame is unitary and
##{\Upsilon^{-1}D_{a,U}\Upsilon
 =D^{\mathrm{st}}_{a,U}\otimes\bbI_S,}
where the tensor factors on the right are ordered by site,
fiber and spin. The staggered operator is skew-adjoint and
odd for site parity.}

\p{If a site-independent self-adjoint grading #{\Gamma} on
#{S\otimes F} anticommutes with every
#{\varepsilon_j^S\otimes\bbI_F}, then pointwise
##{\Upsilon^{-1}\Gamma\Upsilon=(-1)^{|x|}\Gamma.}
This includes the even-dimensional volume grading #{C} of
\ref{fdip-009X}.}

\proof{
\p{Each generator is a unitary involution, so the ordered
product is unitary. Only the odd exponents contribute.
Multiplying on the left by #{\varepsilon_j^S} moves it past
the generators with indices less than #{j}, acquiring the
factor #{(-1)^{x_1+\cdots+x_{j-1}}}. It cancels the
#{j}-th generator if present and inserts it otherwise.
At either neighbor #{x\pm e_j}, exactly the #{j}-th parity
changes, also across the seam because #{N_j} is even. Thus
##{\Upsilon_x^{-1}\varepsilon_j^S\Upsilon_{x\pm e_j}
 =(-1)^{x_1+\cdots+x_{j-1}}\bbI_S.}}

\p{At #{x}, the forward input is transported by #{U_j(x)^{-1}}
and the backward one by #{U_j(x-e_j)}. Both maps act on #{F},
so the spin calculation applies to each edge separately.
Taking their difference and dividing by #{2a} gives the
claimed operator on each spin component. The directional sign
is real and constant along its own shift. Hence it commutes
with both shifts, whose difference is skew-adjoint. Their
signed sum is skew-adjoint. Each edge changes site parity, so
conjugation by site parity reverses every summand.}

\p{Passing #{\Gamma} through the #{|x|} generator factors
of #{\Upsilon_x\otimes\bbI_F} gives the sign #{(-1)^{|x|}}.
This proves the pointwise grading identity.}
}

\p{An orthogonal projection on #{S} commutes with the transformed
operator. Conjugating back gives the site projection
#{\Upsilon_x P\Upsilon_x^{-1}} for a fixed spin projection #{P}.
Its range and kernel are invariant, and a rank-one range carries
one copy of the staggered operator. The generator toggle with
its preceding-index sign occurs in TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.ι_mul_basis}.
Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.IsIdempotentElem.commute_iff}
relates commuting projections to the two invariant subspaces.}
